<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lope de Vega</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1562">1562</a>–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Félix Lope de Vega y Carpio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lope de Vega was a Spanish playwright and poet, one of the leading figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a>. Extraordinarily prolific, he is said to have written some 1,500 plays, of which several hundred survive, including Fuenteovejuna and The Dog in the Manger. He shaped the Spanish comedia, the three-act play mixing tragedy and comedy that dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega.jpg" alt="Lope de Vega" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Eugenio Caxés · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LopedeVega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
